                <h2>Conclusion First: Don't Split Budget Evenly During Cold-Start</h2>
<p>Many cross-border independent site teams make a fatal mistake in early ad投放: dividing the budget into four or five parts for Facebook, Google, TikTok, SEO, and influencer marketing respectively. The result is that no channel accumulates enough data to calculate a clear ROI.</p>
<p>My view is: <strong>During cold-start, at least 60% of the budget should be concentrated on the fastest-acting single channel</strong> to verify whether the "traffic-conversion-payback" loop can be established. Scattered testing sounds safe, but actually wastes testing costs.</p>
<hr />
<h2>The Logic Behind Budget Concentration</h2>
<p>Independent sites lack natural traffic accumulation unlike Amazon's search weight. Ads are the only startup fuel, but fuel is limited—must concentrate to ignite one flame.</p>
<p><strong>Benefits of concentrated投放:</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Higher data density</strong>: The same daily budget of $500 concentrated on Facebook versus split into $125 per channel creates a massive gap in data samples. The former can accumulate 300-500 click conversions in a week; the latter may have only a few dozen per channel, making it impossible to judge real effectiveness.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Faster algorithm learning</strong>: Meta and Google's ad systems need sufficient conversion data to optimize models. Scattered budgets keep algorithms lingering in learning phase, with inflated CPMs and unstable conversions.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Quicker iteration</strong>: Testing a single channel for 2-3 weeks—if still no positive ROI—likely indicates a product-audience-landing page combination issue rather than the channel itself. Switching channels for retesting is more efficient.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Budget Allocation by Stage</h2>
<h3>Stage 1: Cold-Start (0-4 weeks)</h3>
<p><strong>Goal: Verify minimum profit model</strong></p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Channel</th>
<th>Budget %</th>
<th>Objective</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Primary testing channel (Facebook/Meta recommended)</td>
<td>60-70%</td>
<td>Rapid scaling, accumulating conversion data</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Secondary verification channel (Google Search recommended)</td>
<td>20-30%</td>
<td>Test high-intent keywords</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Backup exploration (TikTok/Social)</td>
<td>10%</td>
<td>Reserve creative assets, no immediate conversion target</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><strong>Implementation tips:</strong> Run 2-3 campaigns with 2-3 ad sets each in the primary channel. Early-stage new accounts face fierce competition; parallel testing helps identify audience response faster.</p>
<h3>Stage 2: Optimization (4-8 weeks)</h3>
<p><strong>Goal: Find 2-3 stable profit-generating channel combinations</strong></p>
<p>When the primary channel shows ROAS ≥ 3 on certain ad groups, the profit model is initially confirmed. At this point:</p>
<ul>
<li>Increase primary channel budget by 30-50%</li>
<li>Migrate winning creatives to secondary channels for testing</li>
<li>Maintain 10-15% budget for continuous new channel testing</li>
</ul>
<p>Caution: Don't rush to increase budget just because one channel shows slight profit. A utility app team once went all-in after Facebook showed ROAS 2.8, only to see CTR drop 40% within two weeks due to creative fatigue—with new account not yet stabilized, leaving them stranded.</p>
<h3>Stage 3: Stable Growth (8+ weeks)</h3>
<p><strong>Goal: Optimize channel mix, control CAC fluctuations</strong></p>
<p>Mature-stage budget allocation has no standard answer—it depends on your margin and LTV:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>High-margin products (&gt;60%)</strong>: Can accept ROAS 2-2.5, focus on scaling</li>
<li><strong>Low-margin products (&lt;40%)</strong>: Need ROAS 4+ for profit, shift budget toward Google Search</li>
<li><strong>High-repeat-purchase categories</strong>: Allocate appropriate budget to TikTok and social seeding for long-term user asset building</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Two Pitfall Warnings</h2>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Pitfall 1: Choosing primary channel by intuition</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Facebook isn't optimal for every category. For products with AOV exceeding $150, Google Search typically delivers 30-50% higher conversion rates because buyer purchase intent is clearer during active search. Conversely, sub-$50 fast-moving goods with impulse-buy attributes convert better on TikTok and Instagram's visual impact.</p>
<p>Recommendation: Test Facebook and Google Search separately with $500-$1,000 each for one week, then decide on primary channel based on CPL comparison.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Pitfall 2: Ignoring minimum budget thresholds</strong></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Meta officially recommends minimum daily budget of $50 per new ad set; Google Search campaigns minimum $20. If your total daily budget is under $200, concentrating on one channel is more reasonable than spreading thin—$150 on Facebook with $50 exploring Google beats $50 per channel where algorithms never learn.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>The core principle for independent site ad budget allocation is <strong>"concentrate first, then diversify."</strong> During cold-start, concentrate resources to verify a single channel's profit model; only expand channel mix after finding ROAS-positive ad groups. Even distribution seems prudent but is actually the slowest path.</p>
<p>Budget allocation is essentially proactive risk management. Betting limited funds on high-confidence channels gets you closer to success than diversifying across all channels.</p>
